rs  on this slide ignore it  but this is to tell you that we  basically trained in  one cohort and validated in two  independent cores  so how does this artificial intelligence  model works it's a prediction and  machine  learning method we call it lung cancer  predictor  convolutional neural network the neural  network is basically  taking a bunch of images like you see in  the ace part of the slide  and bringing them to a computer to have  them recognize  what is a cancer and what is a benign  nodule  and then we train the classifier to at  the end  gives us an answer as to whether the  image we have in front of us is likely  to be cancerous or not and give us a  percentage of  likelihood score of cancer let's say 75  chance that this is a cancer this is how  we train it in b  you see that in the training data set we  provide the  the artificial intelligence model  with three nodules two that are benign  one that's malignant and then  we give them a large number of nodules  and the better  uh the module the the model gets a lot  better with the large number of nodules  so we end up with over 15 000 nodules  and train the model  if the model gets the answer wrong we  retrain the model we'll refine the model  there's an iterative aspect to this  training procedure and at the end we  give  the model unknown nodules and we ask  them tell us what it is  and then we see in what percentage of  the time we are accurate at determining  if this is a cancer  and this is a benign one so here  um i wanted to show you a little video  that  illustrates how we eventually  perform this analysis based on  the images that are selected on our  imaging record  we can actually download directly the  images  and you'll see that we screen through  the images really quickly there is a  nodule in the right upper lobe  we're going to identify and select that  nodule in the right upper lobe find its  center  click on the very center of the nodule  itself  and try to draw a sphere around it  