ical utility study  will tell you  and me whether this is gonna help  patients get their diagnose  done earlier and with fewer procedures  and whether eventually this may save  lives  so we need to get to a  proposal of a clinical trial  and to bring patients and  biomarkers onto a train and the idea  that we are  we are riding this train with our lung  nodules  and we want some bowel markers to be  tested to see whether  we are accurate and clinically useful  we're going to test a bunch of  biomarkers on the train we're going to  take some on it  and we're going to let some of these  biomarkers that are not useful  leave the train and we'll arrive at  destination hopefully with a great  proposal  so how do we propose to do this in a  randomized fashion  what does it mean if you are let's say  60 years old  and you present with a lung nodule  that's in this intermediate risk  we're not totally sure whether it's  benign or not we would randomize you  into two groups a group that has  just a standard of care and we follow  you over time that's our control group  or an intervention group and the  intervention group here  will offer some type of biomarker it  could be a clinical model  a blood viral marker or our imaging  biomarker  and our imaging biomarker will be  applied directly to the chest ct that  you have  that you come to your doctor with we  look at the imaging  we run our artificial intelligence tool  and the tool tells us that no in fact  you're not in the intermediate risk  you are much lower risk you're in the  low risk group  and we can follow you you follow you  safely  with just a chest ct that would be quite  reassuring  if the bowel marker our imaging lung  um artificial intelligence  model tells us no your nigel is actually  pretty high risk  we rather you get a biopsy we'll follow  these  strategies get the biopsy that your  doctors decide  on getting and eventually look at the  end point and in this trial within two  years we'll know whether  we'r